# Built-in shortcodes

@lede
The shortcodes the reference compiler ships with. Implementations may not
redefine these names.
@end

## At a glance

@t
| Name | Form | Purpose
| `link` | inline | Hyperlink — `@link[text](url)`
| `image` | inline | Image — `@image(src: "...", alt: "...")`
| `kbd` | inline | Keyboard input — `@kbd[Ctrl+C]`
| `sub` | inline | Subscript — `@sub[2]`
| `sup` | inline | Superscript — `@sup[2]`
| `details` | block | Collapsible block — `@details(summary: "...")`
| `dl` | block | Definition list — `@dl ... @end`
| `t` | block | Table (special-cased syntax)
| `code` | block | Same as triple-backtick fence
| `callout` | block | Boxed note — kind is one of `note`, `tip`, `important`, `warning`, `caution`
| `math` | both | LaTeX math, inline or block
| `footnote` | inline | Footnote — `@footnote[...]`
| `ref` | inline | Cross-document reference — `@ref[path#anchor](title)`

## `@link` — hyperlinks

```brief
See @link[the spec](https://example.com/brief) for details.
```

The body is the display text. The first positional argument is the URL
(required). Optional keyword: `title:` for the `title` HTML attribute.

```brief
@link[Anthropic](https://anthropic.com, title: "Anthropic homepage")
```

## `@image` — images

```brief
@image(src: "diagram.png", alt: "architecture diagram")[]
```

Required keyword: `src`. Optional keyword: `alt`. The body must be empty
brackets — images don't have inline children.

In LLM mode the rendering is `[image: <alt>]`. URL is dropped unless
`--keep-asset-urls` is passed.

## `@kbd` — keyboard input

```brief
Press @kbd[Ctrl+C] to abort.
```

Renders as a styled `<kbd>` element (HTML) or its body verbatim (LLM).

## `@sub` and `@sup` — sub/superscript

```brief
H@sub[2]O
e = mc@sup[2]
```

Replacements for the inline `<sub>` / `<sup>` HTML tags. No arguments;
the body is the text.

## `@details` — collapsible blocks

```brief
@details(summary: "Click to expand")
The body of the collapsed region.

Multiple paragraphs and other blocks work here.
@end
```

Required keyword: `summary`. The HTML rendering is
`<details><summary>...</summary>...</details>`.

## `@dl` — definition lists

```brief
@dl
Term 1
: Definition of term 1.
Term 2
: Definition of term 2.
@end
```

The `:` marker is exact — colon plus exactly one space. See
@ref[language/definition-lists.brf](Definition lists).

## `@t` — tables

Self-terminating block shortcode. Opens with `@t` (and optional
`(align: [...])` argument), then one or more `|`-prefixed rows, then ends
at the first non-`|` line. Full reference:
@ref[language/tables.brf](Tables).

## `@code` — code blocks

Equivalent to a triple-backtick fence. The first positional argument is
the language tag.

```brief
@code(rust)
fn main() {
    println!("hi");
}
@end
```

Use the fence form by default; `@code` exists for parity with the
shortcode model.

## `@callout` — boxed notes

```brief
@callout(kind: warning)
Watch out for the @kbd[Shift+Delete] shortcut.
@end
```

Required keyword: `kind`. Allowed values: `note`, `tip`, `important`,
`warning`, `caution`. The first three plus the last two match GFM's
alert kinds.

In LLM mode the rendering is `[!<kind>] ... [/!]`.

## `@math` — LaTeX math

Inline:

```brief
The energy is @math[E = m c^2].
```

Block:

```brief
@math
\sum_{i=1}^{n} i = \frac{n(n+1)}{2}
@end
```

The body is verbatim LaTeX, passed through to the HTML target inside
`<span class="math">` (inline) or `<div class="math math-display">`
(block). LLM mode renders inline math as `$body$` and block math as
`$$body$$`.

Brief does not ship a LaTeX engine. Wire one up at the page-template
layer (KaTeX, MathJax) for browser rendering.

## `@footnote` — footnotes

The footnote *body* lives next to the reference, not at the bottom of
the file:

```brief
This claim is contested.@footnote[See Smith, 2025.]
```

The compiler auto-numbers footnotes in document order and emits a list at
the end of the page. HTML rendering puts them in `<ol class="footnotes">`;
LLM rendering uses `[^N]: ...` lines.

This is the *only* form. No reference-style separated definitions —
Brief is context-free, and a forward-declared footnote definition would
require a multi-pass parser for one feature.

## `@ref` — cross-document references

```brief
See @ref[guide.brf#install](installation) for the steps.
```

The body is the target — a project-relative `.brf` path, optionally with
a `#anchor` suffix. The first positional argument is the display text.

Validation:

- Missing target file: @ec[B0601].
- Missing anchor in target file: @ec[B0602].
- Malformed target (leading `/`, `..` segments, backslashes): @ec[B0603].
- No `brief.toml` walking up from the source: @ec[B0604].

In LLM mode the link is dropped — only the display text remains.

## What's not a built-in

`@br` for hard breaks. Use a trailing `\` instead. `@hr` for horizontal
rules. Use `---` instead. Both are explicit refusals — adding a second
spelling would break the "one canonical form" rule.
